!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/34/ltr8/cli/diagnostics-11.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/34/m/meta.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/34/m/core.tn"
@doc:"""
  This project's own schema !!id convention (see CLAUDE.md's "Project-owned schema !!id
  convention"): /2026/34 is the spec revision this schema is written against, ltr8 is the
  publishing org, cli is this module's own schema group, and the trailing -11 is this schema's
  own version.

  That version is bumped on a *release*, not on a change. [TSON-SCHEMA] §10's immutability rule
  binds a published identity: once a release of this library ships carrying this schema, the
  document under this !!id is fixed, and a later shape change mints -12 rather than editing it.
  Between releases -- while the build version carries -SNAPSHOT -- nothing has published this
  identity, so this document is in development and is edited in place. Bumping per change instead
  mints versions nobody ever consumed, one for every field added during a development cycle.

  diagnostic_code and fetch_reason are hand-written copies of Diagnostic.Code and
  TsonSchemaFetchException.Reason. DiagnosticsSchemaTest checks both copies against the Java enums
  in both directions, which is what catches a member added to one and forgotten here -- before that
  test existed, two such omissions were noticed only by a version bump.

  BIND_MISMATCH is not reachable from this CLI, which reads in DOM mode and binds nothing. It is
  declared because this enum copies Diagnostic.Code, not the subset one consumer happens to emit
  -- the same reason DUPLICATE_FIELD's absence was a defect rather than an economy.
  """
{
  diagnostic_code => !enum [
    FIELD_REQUIRED FIELD_FIXED TYPE_MISMATCH WRONG_ARITY UNKNOWN_TYPE_REF ATOM_CONSTRAINT_VIOLATION
    UNRECOGNIZED_FIELD DUPLICATE_MAP_KEY DUPLICATE_FIELD CONFUSABLE_NAMES RESTRICTED_TOKEN SCHEMA_ERROR
    UNKNOWN_TYPE VALIDATION_ERROR NOT_IMPLEMENTED BIND_MISMATCH SCHEMA_UNAVAILABLE
  ]
  @doc:"""
    Why a schema could not be obtained, carried only beside SCHEMA_UNAVAILABLE. The code says that no
    schema was obtained; this says by whose doing, which is the half a caller acts on: NOT_PERMITTED
    names a reference this deployment will not fetch and NOT_FOUND one nothing serves, both the
    document's to fix, where TRANSPORT, TIMEOUT and TOO_LARGE say the reference was fine and the
    world was not, and only those are worth retrying.
    """
  fetch_reason => !enum [ NOT_PERMITTED NOT_FOUND TRANSPORT TIMEOUT TOO_LARGE ]
  @doc:"""
    One problem, located at up to two ends: the value in the data (path, data_position) and the rule
    in the schema (schema_id, schema_pointer, schema_position). Either end may be absent -- a problem
    found in a schema has no data, and a schemaless read has no schema.

    Absence is written as absence: every field that may have nothing to say is optional here and
    renders as null in --output json. That includes both RFC 6901 pointers, and the distinction they
    draw is load-bearing: a present "" is the root, which a document-level schema problem genuinely
    carries, while null means this diagnostic has no such end at all.

    A position is "line:column:byte_offset", all three 1-based except byte_offset, which counts
    UTF-8 bytes from 0 ([TSON-DATA] §8.1).
    """
  diagnostic => {
    path: text?
    schema_pointer: text?
    schema_id: text?
    code: diagnostic_code
    message: text
    expected: text?
    actual: text?
    data_position: text?
    schema_position: text?
    fetch_reason: fetch_reason?
  }
  validation_report => { valid: boolean  errors: [diagnostic] }
  file_report => { file: text  valid: boolean  errors: [diagnostic] }
  validation_run => { valid: boolean  files: [file_report]  errors: [diagnostic] }
}
